<article class="env-warning" id="057J">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/057J">Warning <span data-tag="057J">4.7.7.6</span>.</a> We have now given two <em>a priori</em> different definitions for the notion of $2$-category: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(a)$</dt>
<dd><p>
According to Definition <a data-tag="009Q" href="/tag/009Q">2.2.8.5</a>, a $2$-category is a bicategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ in which every $2$-morphism is an isomorphism. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(b)$</dt>
<dd><p>
According to Definition <a data-tag="057E" href="/tag/057E">4.7.7.1</a>, a $2$-category is a simplicial set which satisfies some additional conditions. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> However, these definitions are essentially the same. If $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a $2$-category in the sense of $(a)$, then the Duskin nerve $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }^{\operatorname{D}}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ is a $2$-category in the sense of $(b)$ (Example <a data-tag="057H" href="/tag/057H">4.7.7.5</a>). We will later see that the converse is also true: every $2$-category in the sense $(b)$ is isomorphic to the Duskin nerve $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }^{\operatorname{D}}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$, where $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a bicategory in which every $2$-morphism is an isomorphism. Moreover, Theorem <a data-tag="00AU" href="/tag/00AU">2.3.4.1</a> guarantees that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is unique up to non-strict isomorphism. See Proposition <a class="" data-content="This is a reference to something which is not yet available in Kerodon." data-html="true" data-placement="bottom" data-toggle="popover" data-trigger="focus" role="button" style="cursor: pointer;" tabindex="0" title="Future reference"><img src="/static/images/question.svg"/></a>. </p>
</article>
